1. List (Ordered, duplicates allowed)
1) ArrayList
Dynamic array, fast for reading, slow for insert/delete
Use when: You need fast access & mostly read operations
Syntax:
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();

2) LinkedList
Doubly linked list, fast insert/delete, slow access
Use when: Frequent add/remove in middle
Syntax:
List<String> list = new LinkedList<>();

3) Vector (Legacy)
Synchronized ArrayList
Use when: Thread-safe dynamic array needed
Syntax:
Vector<String> v = new Vector<>();

4) Stack (Legacy)
LIFO (Last In First Out)
Use when: Use stack operations (push/pop)
Syntax:
Stack<Integer> st = new Stack<>();

2. Set (No duplicates)
1) HashSet
No order, fast, unique elements
Use when: Need unique items, order doesn’t matter
Syntax:
Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();

2.LinkedHashSet
Maintains insertion order
Use when: Unique + preserve order
Syntax:
Set<String> set = new LinkedHashSet<>();

3) TreeSet
Sorted set (ascending), unique
Use when: Need sorting + uniqueness
Syntax:
Set<String> set = new TreeSet<>();

3. Queue (FIFO)
1) PriorityQueue
Elements ordered by priority (min-heap by default)
Use when: Need highest/lowest priority element first
Syntax:
Queue<Integer> q = new PriorityQueue<>();

2) LinkedList (as Queue)
FIFO queue
Use when: Simple queue operations
Syntax:
Queue<String> q = new LinkedList<>();

3)ArrayDeque
Faster than LinkedList for queue + stack
Use when: Need both queue and stack behaviour
Syntax:
Deque<String> dq = new ArrayDeque<>();

4. Map (Key–Value pairs)
1) HashMap
Fast, no order
Use when: Key-value storage without order
Syntax:
Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

2) LinkedHashMap
Maintains insertion order
Use when: Need predictable iteration order
Syntax:
Map<String, Integer> map = new LinkedHashMap<>();

| Need                     | Use           |
| ------------------------ | ------------- |
| Random access            | ArrayList     |
| Fast insert/delete       | LinkedList    |
| Unique items, no order   | HashSet       |
| Unique + insertion order | LinkedHashSet |
| Unique + sorted          | TreeSet       |
| Key-value                | HashMap       |
| Key-value + order        | LinkedHashMap |
| Key-value + sorted       | TreeMap       |
| Thread-safe List         | Vector        |
| Thread-safe Map          | Hashtable     |
| Priority-based queue     | PriorityQueue |
| Fast queue/stack         | ArrayDeque    |


| Collection    | Add Syntax                         | Remove Syntax                               |
| ------------- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| ArrayList     | `add()`                            | `remove()`, `remove(index)`                 |
| LinkedList    | `add()`, `addFirst()`, `addLast()` | `remove()`, `removeFirst()`, `removeLast()` |
| Vector        | `add()`                            | `remove()`                                  |
| Stack         | `push()`                           | `pop()`                                     |
| HashSet       | `add()`                            | `remove()`                                  |
| LinkedHashSet | `add()`                            | `remove()`                                  |
| TreeSet       | `add()`                            | `remove()`                                  |
| PriorityQueue | `add()`, `offer()`                 | `remove()`, `poll()`                        |
| ArrayDeque    | `add()`, `addFirst()`              | `remove()`, `removeFirst()`                 |
| HashMap       | `put()`                            | `remove(key)`                               |
| LinkedHashMap | `put()`                            | `remove(key)`                               |
| TreeMap       | `put()`                            | `remove(key)`                               |
| Hashtable     | `put()`                            | `remove(key)`                               |

3) TreeMap
Sorted by key (ascending)
Use when: Need sorted map
Syntax:
Map<String, Integer> map = new TreeMap<>();

4) Hashtable (Legacy)
Thread-safe HashMap
Use when: Need synchronized key-value store
Syntax:
Hashtable<String, Integer> ht = new Hashtable<>();



